  "text": "Whether the Celts took up a belief in reincarnation stays open to debate. The term reserves a soul for a fresh body in each life, with the deeds of one life falling on the next. The Hindu school calls that path karma. When a soul may rest in a bird or a beast, the better word is the migration of souls. Julius Caesar wrote that the Druids taught a fresh life for the soul in every death. That teaching freed a warrior to battle without fear, since another body waited. Caesar sought the Celtic lands at that same time, so his account carries a bias. Diodorus of Sicily set the Celtic faith beside the rebirth teaching of Pythagoras. A few Celtic heroes show a return in a later form, among them Étain, Tuan Mac Cairill, Fintan, Friuch, and Taliesin. These cases may mark a divine spark, not a law for every person. The Celts also pictured an Otherworld where the old and the fresh share one place with their dead.",
